#use(std/code)
#use(std/table)

#section{Bodies}::

A call carries its content as an inline brace body, several brace bodies, or
a rest-of-line body. The body is markup by default, parsed to Content and
passed as the call's last argument(s).

Inline #code|{{body}}|, multi-body #code|{#row{a}{b}}| (each brace group is
one positional argument), and #code{: rest-of-line}:

#code(lang: "plain")
	#emph{this is emphasized}

	#table
		#row{Name}{Role}
		#row{Kiril}{infra}

	#emph: short emphasized text to end of line

Live:

#emph{this is emphasized}

#table
	#row{Name}{Role}
	#row{Kiril}{infra}

#emph: short emphasized text to end of line

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: Brace body XOR colon body: #code|{#name{a}: b}| is not two bodies — a call takes one form or the other.
	#item: Braces must balance inside #code|{{body}}|; for unbalanced content use a fence (see the fences page).
	#item: A call with an inline body still takes an indented block when it is alone on its line (#code|{#section{Title}}| over a block); a trailing #code{:} is optional — see the indented-blocks page.
	#item: Multi-body works for any function taking several content arguments (#code{#row}, #code{#section}, even #code{#emph}).
	#item: There must be exactly one space after the #code{:} introducer; the formatter normalizes extra spaces there.
